\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Borel sets and finite coordinate products}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{For every finite real field space,
##{\mathcal B(X_V)=\Sigma_V.}
Here #{\Sigma_V} is the [coordinate-product sigma-algebra](fdip-002D).
This finite-coordinate assertion, including dimension zero, follows by
the direct argument below; compare
\citet{Exercises 1.7.18-1.7.19, pp. 194-195, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.}
\proof{
\p{Every evaluation is continuous by \ref{fdip-002C}. For fixed #{v},
the subsets of #{\mathbb R} whose evaluation preimages are Borel form
a sigma-algebra containing all open sets, hence all Borel sets. Thus
each cylinder is Borel and #{\Sigma_V\subseteq\mathcal B(X_V)}.}
\p{Rational open intervals form a countable basis of #{\mathbb R}.
Their finite coordinate products form a countable basis of #{X_V}.
Each is a finite intersection of cylinder sets and belongs to #{\Sigma_V}.
An arbitrary open set is the union of the basis elements it contains;
this is a countable union, proving the reverse inclusion. For empty
#{V}, the one basic box is the singleton itself, and both sigma-algebras
are #{\{\varnothing,X_V\}}.}
}
\p{Mathlib's \leanref{Pi.borelSpace} requires countable indices,
second-countable topological factors and their Borel measurable structures.
Finite real products meet these assumptions. The less restrictive
\leanref{pi_le_borel_pi} gives only one inclusion, not the displayed equality.}
